WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.189
MEGAN ANSDELL: OK.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:09.660
Hello, everybody.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.080
I'm very excited to be
here to share with you all

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.030
about the NASA Frontier
Development Lab

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:17.180
and its partnership
with Google Cloud.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.410
So my name is Megan Ansdell.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.110
I'm a postdoctoral
fellow at UC Berkeley

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.210
in the astronomy department.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:27.860
My background is in astrophysics
and planetary science.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.960
And today, I'm going
to tell you about FDL.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.480
I participated in FDL
last year in 2018.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.980
So the NASA Frontier
Development Lab

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.550
is a public private
partnership between NASA

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:43.150
and partners in industry
that specialize in space

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.610
and artificial intelligence.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:49.690
And the goal of NASA FDL is
to create innovative solutions

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.540
to problems that are of
interest not just to NASA,

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.500
but to humanity in general.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.760
So FDL has focused on problems
like planetary defense,

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.640
so trying to find asteroids
that might be on a collision

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.670
course with Earth before they
hit us so that we don't end up

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.770
like the dinosaurs.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.050
Also looking at problems
like space weather

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.870
so trying to track
flares on our sun

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.810
so that we can better understand
the impacts to satellites

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:18.250
in orbit that we depend a lot
on, like our GPS satellites.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.260
And the way this
works is that FDL

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.340
puts on an eight-week research
accelerator down at the SETI

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.550
Institute in Silicon
Valley over the summer.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.080
And the way it
works is that there

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.000
are these four-person teams.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.940
The teams are made up of two
space science subject matter

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.430
experts and two machine
learning researchers.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.940
And they work very intensely
together for these eight weeks

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.810
on a challenge that's
been given to them.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:47.130
So this past year for FDL,
there were five challenge areas.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.940
It was space resources, space
weather, astrobiology, earth

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.970
observation, and exoplanets.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.590
And each one of
these challenge areas

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.500
was supported by one of
our private partners.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.300
And these private
partners are really

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.490
leaders in commercial AI.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.920
So Google Cloud, of
course, but also companies

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.290
like KX, IBM, NVIDIA, and Intel.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:11.290
And we also had support
from some of the leaders

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.620
in private space, so companies
like Lockheed Martin and Space

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.430
Resources.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.532
So this is the 2018
NASA FDL cohort.

00:02:20.532 --> 00:02:21.990
We're all smiling
and looking fresh

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.940
faced because this is the
beginning of the summer.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.040
We looked very much more
tired at the end of it,

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.862
but for good reasons.

00:02:27.862 --> 00:02:29.820
So we were a very
interdisciplinary and diverse

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:30.320
group.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.250
This is a very special
group of people.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.950
About 50% of us were
based in the United States

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.310
as researchers.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.448
The other 50% were
from around the world.

00:02:39.448 --> 00:02:40.990
A lot of us were
PhD's, so some of us

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:42.990
had PhD's in astrophysics.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.800
Others had PhD's in things
like computer vision, robotics.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.922
It was a wide range.

00:02:47.922 --> 00:02:49.380
There are also
people from industry

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.840
that came from companies
like Hyperloop and Apple,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.648
Audi, just to name a few.

00:02:53.648 --> 00:02:55.440
And it's really because
of our partnerships

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.780
with companies like Google
Cloud that we could all

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.970
come together for
the summer and work

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.010
on these really
exciting challenges

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.710
through this very unique
program of NASA FDL.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.560
So we also had some pretty
amazing data sets to work with.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.215
Sometimes NASA gave
us these data sets.

00:03:13.215 --> 00:03:15.090
Other times, we had to
generate the data sets

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:19.470
using the wonderful compute
power tools through companies

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.120
like Google Cloud.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.850
This particular data set was
used by the space weather team

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.260
this last year.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.500
This is a real image of our sun
taken by NASA Solar Dynamics

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.410
Observatory, which is in
Earth orbit right now.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.585
And it looks for
activity on the sun

00:03:33.585 --> 00:03:38.745
so that we can better understand
the impact of that on Earth.

00:03:38.745 --> 00:03:40.120
So to analyze
these data sets, we

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.840
had access to a very diverse AI
toolkit and just vast amounts

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.350
of compute power,
again, provided to us

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.600
by our private partners.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.060
And I have to say, so I'm
a scientist in academia,

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:53.200
and this was truly amazing.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.770
We do not have access to
resources like this normally.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:59.290
And it was very eye opening
to see what kind of science

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:01.520
could be done in an incredibly
short amount of time

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.317
when you're given just the best
resources that are available.

00:04:06.317 --> 00:04:08.150
So today, I'm going to
talk to you about two

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.120
of the challenges from last
year's NASA FDL program

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.130
that were made possible
through this partnership

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:13.940
with Google Cloud.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:18.750
So that is the planet challenge
and the astrobiology challenge.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.750
So I'll talk about the
exoplanet challenge first

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:25.520
because that was a team that I
was fortunate enough to be on.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.260
So this is the exoplanet team.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.720
So I was one of the
subject matter experts

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:30.920
as a planetary scientist.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.720
Again, I'm at UC Berkeley.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.950
Hugh Osborne was the
other planetary scientist.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.320
He's in Marseilles.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.110
And then the two
deep learning experts

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.670
were Yani Ioannou,
who just graduated

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:44.020
from the University at Cambridge
with a PhD, Michele Sasdelli,

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:45.800
who was a recovering
astrophysicist

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.110
and is now at the
University of Adelaide

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.693
with their computer
vision group.

00:04:50.693 --> 00:04:53.110
We had some great mentors,
both on the science and machine

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.720
learning side, and of
course, our compute resources

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:59.720
were provided by Google Cloud.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.530
OK, so the challenge
that we had was

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.320
to increase the efficacy and
yield of exoplanet transit

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.193
classification
with deep learning.

00:05:07.193 --> 00:05:08.860
So I'm going to break
that down for you,

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:12.710
and I'll start with
the exoplanet side.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.490
So first of all,
what is an exoplanet?

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:17.343
So everybody knows
our solar system.

00:05:17.343 --> 00:05:18.010
There's the sun.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.450
The sun is a star.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:22.360
There are eight
planets in orbit.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.980
No boos about Pluto not
being a planet anymore?

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:25.480
All right.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.350
There are eight-- there you go.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:28.850
So there are eight
planets in orbit.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:30.730
And so an exoplanet
is just a planet

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.800
that's orbiting another star.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.300
There are 100 billion
stars in our galaxy.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:38.085
And we can now say
that on average,

00:05:38.085 --> 00:05:39.460
every single one
of them contains

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.065
at least one exoplanet.

00:05:41.065 --> 00:05:42.940
And that's pretty amazing
that I can stand up

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.060
here and say this today.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.290
So the first exoplanet
was discovered in 1995.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.710
So exoplanet science is
actually an incredibly new field

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:52.300
in astrophysics.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.752
And we now know that we've
detected thousands of them,

00:05:54.752 --> 00:05:56.710
and we can start studying
them as a population.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:59.050
We can start doing
things like estimating

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:02.772
the frequency of Earth-like
planets around sun-like stars.

00:06:02.772 --> 00:06:04.480
And one of the reasons
why we can do that

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.900
is because of the wonderful
success of a particular method

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.400
for detecting
exoplanets, and that's

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:10.840
called the transit method.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:13.180
That's what's being
shown on the left here.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.603
So the transit method works
by just taking advantage

00:06:16.603 --> 00:06:18.520
of the fact that if you
see an exponent system

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.460
at a particular orientation,
when a planet goes in front

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:23.920
of its host star, you see a
drop in brightness of that host

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:24.760
star.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:25.900
And that should be
a periodic thing.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:26.890
Every time the
planet goes around,

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:28.660
you should see a
drop in brightness.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:29.830
So we don't actually
resolve what's

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:31.060
being shown on the
far left there.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.977
We don't actually see
the star and the planet.

00:06:32.977 --> 00:06:36.087
What we do is what's
in the middle there,

00:06:36.087 --> 00:06:37.670
we measure the
brightness of the star.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:39.545
And we just wait for
that drop in brightness.

00:06:39.545 --> 00:06:42.258
And we wait for a repeating
drop in brightness.

00:06:42.258 --> 00:06:44.800
So one of the reasons why the
transit method is so successful

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:47.180
is because of a mission
called the Kepler spacecraft.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.960
How many people here have
heard of the Kepler spacecraft?

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:52.000
That's fantastic.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.933
So Kepler was in space
for about 10 years,

00:06:54.933 --> 00:06:56.350
but actually, its
primary mission,

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.900
which gave us all the data we
worked with in this project,

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.710
was only going on
for about four years.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:04.030
And so during this time,
Kepler stared at one position

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.790
in the sky for four
years, and looked

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:09.820
at about half a million stars,
and measured the brightness

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:12.400
of those stars every 30
minutes, and was just waiting

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.650
for these drops in brightness.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:17.230
And through this
method, Kepler was

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:21.347
able to discover over
2,500 exoplanets.

00:07:21.347 --> 00:07:22.430
So this is pretty amazing.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:26.530
This completely revolutionized
the field of exoplanet science

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:30.472
and brought us to
where we are today.

00:07:30.472 --> 00:07:31.680
But Kepler data isn't pretty.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.847
So everything I showed you--
those wonderful plots--

00:07:33.847 --> 00:07:35.250
it looks great.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.540
That's not what we deal
with on a day-to-day basis.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.840
So the top plot here is
what we call a light curve.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:43.050
So this is a particular
star that Kepler looked at

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:44.925
and the brightness that
was measured every 30

00:07:44.925 --> 00:07:47.775
minutes for about 750 days.

00:07:47.775 --> 00:07:48.900
And you see all this stuff.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.033
You see this ramping up.

00:07:50.033 --> 00:07:51.450
You see these drops
in brightness.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:53.310
So some of that is
spacecraft systematic.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.420
Some of it's instrument noise.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.450
Some of it's actual
stellar variability.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:58.920
And only these
little bitty drops

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.170
that are highlighted
by the red triangles

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.788
are the exoplanet transit.

00:08:03.788 --> 00:08:04.830
So this is one exoplanet.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:08.230
You see three transits in
this particular time period.

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:11.093
And so we do a bunch of stuff
to flatten these light curves.

00:08:11.093 --> 00:08:13.510
So on the bottom panel is you
see a flattened light curve.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:15.677
So it's been cleaned of all
these systematic stellar

00:08:15.677 --> 00:08:18.517
variability, and all you
see are the transits.

00:08:18.517 --> 00:08:20.100
But this is actually
pretty nice data.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.617
It gets a lot worse than this.

00:08:21.617 --> 00:08:23.700
In particular, when you
have more than one planet.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.980
So this is a now
famous planetary system

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.487
called the TRAPPIST system.

00:08:27.487 --> 00:08:29.820
And you see when you have
multiple planets going around,

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.590
you start getting
overlapping transits.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:34.530
It's very hard to sort
of tease all this out.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.711
So it's hard to work with this
data and find these exoplanets.

00:08:37.711 --> 00:08:39.919
Another reason why it's hard
to find these exoplanets

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:42.200
is because there's a variety
of false positive signals

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.059
that mimic exoplanet
transits, and we need

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:46.500
to find these and vet them out.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.510
So one of the main
sources of false positives

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:51.890
are eclipsing binaries.

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:54.350
So here, you don't have
a planet orbiting a star.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:56.430
You actually have two
stars orbiting each other.

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.552
One is usually smaller
than the other.

00:08:58.552 --> 00:09:00.260
And so you can get
things that look a lot

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:02.730
like an exoplanet transit.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.333
Particular nasty case are these
background eclipsing binaries.

00:09:05.333 --> 00:09:07.250
So your target star is
actually a single star,

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:08.880
but there's an eclipsing
binary in the background

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.020
that you can't resolve.

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:14.310
And that can actually look a
lot like an exoplanet transit.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.810
And then there are
chance alignments

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.995
of stellar variability,
instrument noise.

00:09:17.995 --> 00:09:19.620
The example that I
showed before, there

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.155
were three transits.

00:09:21.155 --> 00:09:22.780
And you can imagine
that in some cases,

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.655
you get chance alignment
of three things that

00:09:24.655 --> 00:09:27.420
look like they're on
a periodic cadence.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.237
And so you get these
false positives that way.

00:09:30.237 --> 00:09:32.820
And that becomes a problem when
you dig into the lowest signal

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:34.693
to noise transits,
which unfortunately,

00:09:34.693 --> 00:09:37.110
usually correspond to the most
interesting cases of things

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:38.818
like Earth-like planets
inhabitable zones

00:09:38.818 --> 00:09:41.810
around sun-like stars, which
was the whole point of Kepler.

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:45.380
So you have to vet for
all these false positives.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.020
So the way we do
this, the Kepler team

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.812
developed this very
sophisticated pipeline,

00:09:48.812 --> 00:09:50.440
and I'll go through
this very quickly,

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:52.107
but this is sort of
how we traditionally

00:09:52.107 --> 00:09:54.647
find exoplanet transits.

00:09:54.647 --> 00:09:56.980
So we start off with what are
called target pixel files.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:59.260
These are little postage
stamp images of stars.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.465
This is what's actually
downloaded from the spacecraft.

00:10:02.465 --> 00:10:04.340
Then we use what we call
aperture photometry,

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:05.110
so you put down an aperture.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:06.680
You sum all the light
within that aperture,

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:07.990
and that's how
bright the star is.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:09.460
You do that as a
function of time,

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:11.500
and you get those light curves
that I showed you before that

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.030
have all the systematics in it.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:14.753
You flatten those.

00:10:14.753 --> 00:10:16.420
Then you do an exoplanet
transit search,

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.890
so you start looking
for these transits,

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:19.890
and you record everything
above a certain signal

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:22.100
to noise threshold.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:24.090
You pass that through a
data validation stage.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.363
So this is just-- you start
fitting transit models.

00:10:26.363 --> 00:10:28.280
You start calculating
statistics that can help

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:30.305
you identify false positives.

00:10:30.305 --> 00:10:31.680
And this is an
iterative process.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.972
So you find one transit, you
block out the light curve,

00:10:33.972 --> 00:10:36.260
you send it back into the
transit planet search.

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:38.750
Because as we know
from our solar system,

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:42.230
stars can host many planets.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:45.103
Then when you're done, you feed
all of this to a set of humans.

00:10:45.103 --> 00:10:45.770
Humans sit down.

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:47.728
These are people's jobs,
which is kind of cool.

00:10:47.728 --> 00:10:49.970
Their job is to just
find exoplanets.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:52.580
So they sit around, and they
look at hundreds of thousands

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:53.647
of these transit events.

00:10:53.647 --> 00:10:55.730
And they figure out which
ones are real exoplanets

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:56.500
and which are not.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:58.250
And this is a very
time-consuming process.

00:10:58.250 --> 00:10:59.792
You're sifting
through a lot of data.

00:10:59.792 --> 00:11:01.640
It took years in
the case of Kepler.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.530
But at the end of the day, we
get these wonderful exoplanet

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:05.790
catalogs, and we can start
answering questions like,

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.780
how common is our
Earth in the galaxy?

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:11.030
But this last part, this
last very time-consuming part

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:14.030
is where deep learning can
help and where it already

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:15.290
is helping.

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:18.740
And today, I'm going to tell
you about how we did that.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:21.240
So now I'll move on to the deep
learning side of what we did

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:25.748
and in particular, how Google
Cloud helped us do this.

00:11:25.748 --> 00:11:27.790
So I'm going to give you
a 30 second introduction

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:30.800
to deep learning for people who
aren't too familiar about it.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.180
So deep learning is a
subset of machine learning.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.480
It's called deep
learning because it

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:37.580
uses a series of
computational layers

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:39.830
to basically take
inputs, translate them

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.470
into more useful outputs
by learning increasingly

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:44.987
complex features in the data.

00:11:44.987 --> 00:11:46.820
Neural networks are a
type of deep learning.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:49.340
They're called neural networks
because their architecture

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.813
sort of is loosely analogous
to the neurons in our brain.

00:11:52.813 --> 00:11:54.230
And then convolutional
neural nets

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:55.250
are a type of
neural network that

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:57.460
takes advantage of spatial
features in the data.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:59.360
And they're particularly good at
classification problems, which

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:00.650
is essentially what we're doing.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:02.942
We're saying is this a planet,
or is this not a planet.

00:12:04.973 --> 00:12:06.890
So the reason why we
want to use deep learning

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:09.650
for exoplanet transit science,
there's a bunch of them.

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:11.630
So one, it's quick.

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:12.510
You train a model.

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.468
And then you can classify
hundreds of thousands

00:12:14.468 --> 00:12:17.690
of candidate planet transits
within seconds, instead of

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:19.370
years.

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:21.530
It's systematic, so
this human vetting

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:23.550
that I talked to you
about, it's full of biases

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:24.592
that you can't tease out.

00:12:24.592 --> 00:12:26.570
So I didn't have
coffee this morning.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:28.220
I saw an eclipsing
binary the last time

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.170
I tried to vet a candidate.

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:33.050
I've seen planets the last 10
times I've looked at planets.

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:35.720
All of this stuff biases
your decision making process,

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:37.820
and it's really hard to
tease out those biases

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:38.807
and correct for them.

00:12:38.807 --> 00:12:40.640
And so having a systematic
way of doing this

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:43.250
is really important, and it's
important for calculating

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:46.590
all these exoplanet statistics
that we're interested in.

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:48.090
As we'll see from
this presentation,

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:50.040
there's also easy to
upgrade these models.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:51.990
So you want to
improve your model.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:54.662
It's easy to retrain
and apply to new data.

00:12:54.662 --> 00:12:56.370
And it's quantifiable,
so you're not just

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:57.690
saying this is an exoplanet.

00:12:57.690 --> 00:12:59.040
This is not an exoplanet.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.020
You're saying this is
an exoplanet with 0.8

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:02.750
plus or minus 0.1 probability.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:05.250
And again, you can fold that
into these exoplanet statistics

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:06.583
and make them a lot more robust.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:11.830
So the first people to
actually apply deep learning

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:13.870
to exoplanet transit
classification

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:16.000
was Shallue and Vanderburg 2018.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.160
So Chris Shallue
works at Google Brain.

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:21.607
Andrew Vandenburg is a postdoc
in astrophysics, like myself.

00:13:21.607 --> 00:13:23.190
And they made a model
called Astronet.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:25.000
It's available on
GitHub if you want

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:27.700
to download it and play with the
model in the model architecture

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:29.267
shown on the right.

00:13:29.267 --> 00:13:31.600
But basically, it's a deep
convolutional neural network.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.550
It was written in TensorFlow.

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.830
And the inputs are these
global and local views

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.600
of the phase-folded
candidate transit.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.160
So what I mean by
phase-folded is

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:41.890
that I showed you these light
curves before that spanned four

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:42.610
years.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:44.590
But there's a signal in
there that's periodic.

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:46.630
So you can fold the light
curve on that period,

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:49.060
and you get these nice
exoplanet transits that

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:51.418
are shown on the bottom here.

00:13:51.418 --> 00:13:53.210
So they input these
global and local views.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:55.943
The global views are
important because it

00:13:55.943 --> 00:13:57.110
shows you the whole transit.

00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:59.438
So in the case of these
eclipsing binaries that I told

00:13:59.438 --> 00:14:01.730
you about, when they're two
stars eclipsing each other,

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:03.730
you actually get a primary
and a secondary clip.

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.480
So you get an eclipse
when the smaller

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:07.022
star goes in front
of the bigger star

00:14:07.022 --> 00:14:08.330
and also when it goes behind.

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:09.500
And that's because
you're dealing

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:10.610
with two very bright objects.

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:13.068
You don't see that with a planet
because a planet is small.

00:14:13.068 --> 00:14:15.310
And it doesn't have its
own brightness to it.

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:17.060
So that's important
for identifying things

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:18.860
like eclipsing binaries.

00:14:18.860 --> 00:14:22.190
The local view zooms in
on the transit and maps

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:24.030
out its shape in more detail.

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:27.320
And this is important because
true planet transits actually

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.990
have a pretty unique shape,
the kind of a box-like shape.

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:33.672
That's actually one of the
ways that we can detect them.

00:14:33.672 --> 00:14:35.630
And that's just because
the planet is so small,

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:37.070
compared to the whole star.

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:38.780
So both of these are important.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:42.670
They're fed in as separate
disjoint one-dimensional

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:44.997
convolutional columns
into the model.

00:14:44.997 --> 00:14:46.580
The outputs of the
convolution columns

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:48.658
are flattened and
concatenated, then fed

00:14:48.658 --> 00:14:50.450
through a series of
fully connected layers,

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:51.492
and then a sigmoid layer.

00:14:51.492 --> 00:14:53.940
And the output is this
number between 0 and 1,

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.210
which loosely represents
the likeliness

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.370
of that particular
transit being associated

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:01.615
with a true exoplanet.

00:15:01.615 --> 00:15:03.240
So Astronet did
something really cool--

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:07.380
discover two new super Earths
in two different systems.

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:09.810
Super Earths are really cool
because we don't have them

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:10.720
in our solar system.

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:13.230
So if you imagine our solar
system was shown on the bottom

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:16.823
here, we have the rocky
planets in our solar system.

00:15:16.823 --> 00:15:18.240
And then we have
the big gassy ice

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.685
giants on the outer solar
system and nothing in between.

00:15:20.685 --> 00:15:22.560
So supers are cool
because we don't have them

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:24.150
in our own solar system.

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:26.160
They're also really cool
because it turns out

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.440
what we know from Kepler
now is that they're by far,

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:31.840
the most common type of
planet in the galaxy.

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:34.660
So they're important and we
need to learn about them.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:37.240
So the Kepler 90 system
was also a sun-like star,

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:39.600
so it was a super Earth
around a sun-like star.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:40.900
And it brought that system.

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.048
It brought it up to eight
planets in the system.

00:15:44.048 --> 00:15:46.590
So it's actually the system in
our galaxy with the most known

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:48.627
exoplanets, and it
brings it up a tie

00:15:48.627 --> 00:15:49.710
with our own solar system.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.200
So what we did over
the summer through FDL

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:58.650
was to upgrade Astronet
with what we call added

00:15:58.650 --> 00:15:59.890
scientific domain knowledge.

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:01.682
So it's just information
that us scientists

00:16:01.682 --> 00:16:04.020
had that could improve
the model performance.

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:06.385
And it ended up improving
it for, in particular,

00:16:06.385 --> 00:16:08.760
the low signal to noise transits
that correspond to Earth

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:10.660
sized planets.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.850
And we called our
model Exonet, and it's

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:15.670
also available on GitLab if you
want to check the model out.

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:18.505
The updated architecture's
on the right.

00:16:18.505 --> 00:16:19.880
But what I wanted
to do, before I

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:21.770
get into the science
side of it, was just

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:24.020
highlight the role
of Google Cloud.

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:27.335
So Google Cloud was really
sort of the glue that held

00:16:27.335 --> 00:16:28.460
the whole project together.

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:31.160
So through it, we could
make our code in Python

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:33.650
through a collaborative
environment of the Jupyter

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:34.850
notebooks.

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:36.650
We called on PyTorch
and scikit-learn

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:39.030
to build, and test,
and train our models.

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:41.480
And the whole thing was
incredibly seamless.

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:43.910
And I walked into
the FDL program

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:46.250
with no machine learning
experience, very little

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:48.770
collaborative coding
experience, and Google Cloud

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:53.930
made the whole thing incredibly
easy to learn and to pick up.

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:56.300
Each of our team members
had custom instances.

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:57.950
We had our own virtual machines.

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:01.160
So we could sort of customize
these machines to whatever

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.950
we happened to be doing at that
moment, whether it was building

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:06.410
the model, or training
it, or testing it.

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:09.829
We had access to
amazing GPU resources.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:11.990
And we used a persistent
solid state drive

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:15.030
for reading our data
quickly for training.

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:17.490
And so again, I just want
to highlight as an academic,

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:19.452
this was absolutely amazing.

00:17:19.452 --> 00:17:21.910
Let me give you some insight
into how it works in academia.

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:24.450
So we typically
apply for a grant

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:27.690
to NASA or the National
Science Foundation.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.648
And sometimes those grants
let us buy hardware.

00:17:29.648 --> 00:17:31.440
And so we say we want
to buy this hardware.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:33.540
It's usually not v100's,
because then we would not

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:36.100
get the grant approved.

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:37.560
But you get some
money for hardware

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:39.352
if you're lucky enough,
and then you go off

00:17:39.352 --> 00:17:40.628
and you buy that hardware.

00:17:40.628 --> 00:17:43.170
And then you use that hardware
for on the order of five to 10

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:44.682
years, sometimes longer.

00:17:44.682 --> 00:17:46.890
And then you get another
grant, and the whole process

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:48.730
starts over again.

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:52.150
So it's very slow, and so our
resources are not just limited,

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:53.670
but they're out of date.

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:56.778
So to have the resources that
are provided by Google Cloud

00:17:56.778 --> 00:17:57.570
was pretty amazing.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:03.060
OK, so what did we add to our
model to increase the model

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:05.140
performance?

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:07.020
So one of the things
we added first

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:08.620
was a centroid time-series.

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:10.380
So I showed the
light curves before,

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:13.383
which is just how bright the
star is as a function of time.

00:18:13.383 --> 00:18:15.050
The other thing you
get from this data--

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:16.440
so I mentioned before
what we actually

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.760
get from the spacecraft are
these postage stamp images.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:21.450
So you can lay down an aperture
and measure the brightness

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:22.590
within that aperture.

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:25.260
But you can also measure the
pixel position of the center

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:26.758
of light within that aperture.

00:18:26.758 --> 00:18:28.050
And that's called the centroid.

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:29.832
And you get that as
a function of time.

00:18:29.832 --> 00:18:31.290
So in this case,
let's say you have

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:32.783
a background eclipsing binary.

00:18:32.783 --> 00:18:34.950
When that eclipse happens
on that background source,

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:36.325
the centroid will
actually shift.

00:18:36.325 --> 00:18:38.040
It'll shift away
from that binary.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.500
And you can use that information
to identify false positives.

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:42.847
So this is what I mean.

00:18:42.847 --> 00:18:44.430
So again, these
global and local views

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:46.918
of the phase-folded transit.

00:18:46.918 --> 00:18:48.210
On the top is a real exoplanet.

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:50.252
So you see the transit,
but you don't see a shift

00:18:50.252 --> 00:18:52.462
in the centroid,
which is shown in red.

00:18:52.462 --> 00:18:54.420
On the bottom is a
background eclipsing binary,

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:56.040
and you can see in
the transit, you

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.420
get the shift in the centroid.

00:18:57.420 --> 00:18:59.702
So you can take advantage
of that information.

00:18:59.702 --> 00:19:02.160
And so the way we did that was
to create secondary channels

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:04.032
in the convolutional
columns and to feed

00:19:04.032 --> 00:19:06.240
in the centroid time-series
along with a light curve.

00:19:08.267 --> 00:19:10.350
The other thing we did was
add stellar properties.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.250
So one thing I didn't mention is
that with this transit method,

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:14.780
the planet properties
you get out

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:17.960
actually heavily depend on the
properties of the host star.

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:20.750
So one of the things we did was
tell the model the properties

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:22.172
of that particular host star.

00:19:22.172 --> 00:19:24.380
And there's a variety of
reasons why this might help,

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:26.920
but I'm showing
one of them here.

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:29.740
So here, you have a
giant star being orbited

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:30.760
by a main sequence star.

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:32.135
And I'll explain
what that means.

00:19:32.135 --> 00:19:33.667
So if you have a binary system.

00:19:33.667 --> 00:19:35.500
And by the way, half
the stars in our galaxy

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:36.310
are binary system.

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:38.440
So our sun is
single, but actually,

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:40.190
50% of the stars in
the galaxy are binary.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:42.082
So this is an important effect.

00:19:42.082 --> 00:19:43.540
When you have a
binary system, they

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:44.930
don't evolve at the same rate.

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:46.263
They don't age at the same rate.

00:19:46.263 --> 00:19:48.860
Usually, one ages
quicker than the other.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:51.180
And when happens,
the star blows up.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:52.180
It becomes a giant star.

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:54.490
We call it a giant star
because we have no imagination.

00:19:54.490 --> 00:19:56.185
But it gets really puffy.

00:19:56.185 --> 00:19:58.310
Our sun is going to do
that, and when it does that,

00:19:58.310 --> 00:19:59.920
it will actually
engulf the Earth.

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:01.900
So that's how big these
things are getting.

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:04.150
But you can see the size
difference is actually really

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:05.680
similar to a star and a planet.

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:08.440
So you can get exoplanet
transits or things

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:09.880
that look like
exoplanet transits,

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:13.570
when really, it's just an old
star with a middle aged star

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:14.800
orbiting it.

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:16.762
So we fed in the
stellar parameters.

00:20:16.762 --> 00:20:18.220
We concatenated
them to the outputs

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:20.512
of the convolutional columns
before the fully connected

00:20:20.512 --> 00:20:24.218
layers, and that's how we took
advantage of that information.

00:20:24.218 --> 00:20:25.760
So we were able to
increase the model

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:28.967
performance over the baseline
Astronet by a few percent.

00:20:28.967 --> 00:20:30.800
Astronet was already
performing really well,

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:33.500
so that increase of
just a few percent

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:37.490
actually corresponded to about
50% decreases in model error.

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:38.990
And there's a
precision recall curve

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:41.532
shown on the right that shows
you that the centroids actually

00:20:41.532 --> 00:20:43.970
provided the best boost
to the model performance,

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:47.832
but the stellar
properties also helped.

00:20:47.832 --> 00:20:50.290
What was interesting, though,
is not just the overall model

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:52.810
performance, but it
turns out that we

00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:56.320
were getting much better
recall, almost 20%

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:59.110
higher gains in
recall for exoplanets

00:20:59.110 --> 00:21:00.510
that corresponded to--

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:02.770
that had the lowest transit
signal to noise ratio.

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:05.090
And these correspond
to Earth sized planets.

00:21:05.090 --> 00:21:07.090
So what it's telling us
is that our model Exonet

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:09.688
might be able to find Earth
sized planets still lurking

00:21:09.688 --> 00:21:10.480
in the Kepler data.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:12.272
And that's what we're
working on right now.

00:21:14.328 --> 00:21:15.870
OK, so at the end
of the day, we were

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:18.120
able to show that scientific
domain knowledge improves

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:20.460
exoplanet transit classification
with deep learning.

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:22.560
And all the scientists
let out a big sigh

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:26.220
that their jobs are not being
taken by the machines just yet.

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:28.720
Probably soon, though.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:30.780
OK, so that was the
exoplanet challenge,

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:32.280
and now I'll spend
some time talking

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:33.843
about the astrobiology
challenge.

00:21:33.843 --> 00:21:36.010
My colleague Molly O'Beirne
was supposed to be here.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:37.677
But unfortunately,
she couldn't make it.

00:21:37.677 --> 00:21:39.060
So I'm going to
do the best I can

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:42.560
to share with you their work.

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:44.560
So this is the astrobiology
team from last year.

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:46.018
So Molly O'Beirne
and Michael Himes

00:21:46.018 --> 00:21:47.500
were the planetary scientists.

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:49.990
And Frank Soboczenski
and Simone Zorzan

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:53.183
were the computer scientists.

00:21:53.183 --> 00:21:55.600
They also had wonderful mentors
on the science and machine

00:21:55.600 --> 00:22:00.070
learning side, and of
course, Google Cloud

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:03.320
was the industry partner.

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:04.750
OK.

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:06.128
Are we alone?

00:22:06.128 --> 00:22:08.170
That's a question that's
been asked for hundreds,

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:10.700
if not thousands, of years.

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:13.360
And what's really exciting is
that we're actually at a time

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:15.520
when we might be able
to answer this question.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:18.130
In the near future, we might
be able to detect signatures

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:20.358
of life on another planet.

00:22:20.358 --> 00:22:22.400
So the first step is
finding those other planets,

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:25.380
and as I just showed you, we're
getting pretty good at that.

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.482
But the next step after
that is to figure out

00:22:27.482 --> 00:22:29.690
which one of those planets
is most suitable for life,

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:31.565
and therefore, where we
should look for life.

00:22:31.565 --> 00:22:34.280
Where is the most likely
place that we can detect life?

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:35.760
And that's the hard part.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:37.770
That's still pretty hard.

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:39.540
So the challenge for
the astrobiology team

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:42.000
was to use machine learning
to try and identify

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:44.820
possible extraterrestrial
life on other worlds.

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:46.882
Sounds pretty easy, right?

00:22:46.882 --> 00:22:48.340
But they did have
a starting point.

00:22:48.340 --> 00:22:50.820
So what scientists
think they want to do

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:53.730
is to look at the
atmospheres of exoplanets

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:56.670
and in particular, to look
for molecules or groups

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:59.148
of molecules in those
atmospheres that

00:22:59.148 --> 00:23:00.690
are indicative of
biological activity

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:02.023
on the surface of those planets.

00:23:02.023 --> 00:23:05.323
And so those are collectively
called biosignatures.

00:23:05.323 --> 00:23:06.490
And we can actually do this.

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:10.080
We can detect molecules in
the atmospheres of exoplanets,

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:13.860
but only for these hot Jupiters,
so very massive planets

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:17.110
very close to their star.

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:19.610
And the way we do this
is we get a spectrum.

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:21.250
So we use telescopes
in space to get

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:23.660
a spectrum of the atmosphere.

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:25.780
And that spectrum
has fingerprints

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:27.280
of these molecules.

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:29.350
And we use something called
atmospheric retrieval

00:23:29.350 --> 00:23:32.350
to infer the actual
composition of that atmosphere

00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:34.210
from that spectrum.

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:36.280
The problem is that
atmospheric retrieval--

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:40.540
the traditional way of doing it
is incredibly time-consuming.

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:43.480
So it takes days, if not
weeks, on supercomputers

00:23:43.480 --> 00:23:49.348
to actually back out atmospheric
composition from a spectrum.

00:23:49.348 --> 00:23:51.640
So this is where machine
learning, a different approach

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:54.750
can speed this up.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:57.560
So the astrobiology team
created a code called INARA.

00:23:57.560 --> 00:23:59.690
It stands for the Intelligent
Exoplanet Atmospheric

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:01.280
Retrieval.

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:02.280
So INARA is really cool.

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:05.250
It's an all-in-one
flexible, open source tool.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:07.250
So it starts by generating
planetary spectra

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:10.250
using NASA's Planetary
Spectrum Generator, or PSG.

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:12.287
So the reason why
the astrobiology

00:24:12.287 --> 00:24:14.370
had to generate its data
is what I just mentioned.

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:16.430
We can only measure
exoplanet atmospheres

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:17.840
for these hot Jupiters.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:20.010
For Earth like planets,
it's much more difficult.

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:21.510
We're not quite
there yet, but there

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:24.600
are plans for spacecraft in the
future to be able to do this.

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:27.660
And so this is very
important precursor work.

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:30.470
And so what NASA's Planetary
Spectrum Generator does

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:33.890
is it'll produce a
synthetic spectrum based

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:38.120
on certain spacecraft
configurations and a given

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:40.260
planetary atmosphere.

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:42.740
So they created these spectra.

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:45.950
They can be saved locally
or to a Google Cloud bucket.

00:24:45.950 --> 00:24:48.050
And then there's
other INARA instances

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:50.728
that can pull that data out,
and then on the fly, train,

00:24:50.728 --> 00:24:52.520
and validate, and test
the machine learning

00:24:52.520 --> 00:24:55.952
model and to predict these
planetary atmospheres.

00:24:55.952 --> 00:24:58.160
And this is available as a
Python package or a Docker

00:24:58.160 --> 00:24:59.780
container.

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:02.540
And the astrobiology
team created 3 million

00:25:02.540 --> 00:25:04.880
of these simulated
rocky exoplanet spectra.

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:06.380
And these are
different combinations

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:10.310
of about 28 planetary
and stellar properties.

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:12.300
And it output 12
atmospheric models,

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:13.550
including these biosignatures.

00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:16.190
So this is by far
the largest library

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:18.380
of exoplanet spectra
there is available,

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:22.180
and it's all publicly available.

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:24.480
So this is more of a visual
way of what I just said.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:28.170
So this is the INARA Google
Cloud Platform landscape.

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:30.120
So there was the
data generation size,

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:31.990
and again, 3 million
unique spectra.

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:36.010
And they used 2,000 Google Cloud
virtual machines to do this.

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:37.980
So the way it was
laid out is that there

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:41.190
were these virtual machine
nodes that had the NASA PSG

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.310
tool on them, and then
16 INARA Docker instances

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:45.950
were connected to
each of those nodes,

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:48.340
querying the PSG
to make spectra.

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:50.340
And then those instances
were saving the spectra

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:53.220
to a Google Cloud bucket.

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:54.970
And then there were
separate INARA machine

00:25:54.970 --> 00:25:57.553
learning training instances that
were connected to that Google

00:25:57.553 --> 00:25:59.800
Cloud bucket actively reading
those out and training

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:01.450
the models on the fly.

00:26:01.450 --> 00:26:03.400
And the astrobiology
team did a grid search

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:07.300
of 70 model architectures,
primarily written in PyTorch,

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:09.550
but they also explored
different frameworks using

00:26:09.550 --> 00:26:12.640
[? Karas and ?] TensorFlow.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.530
And what's also
cool about this is

00:26:14.530 --> 00:26:17.060
that if you had a project
that was sort similar to this,

00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:20.290
you can take this framework and
just input your own spectrum

00:26:20.290 --> 00:26:22.632
generator, or what
generator of whatever,

00:26:22.632 --> 00:26:24.590
and then input your own
machine learning model.

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:26.173
And so this whole
framework is set up,

00:26:26.173 --> 00:26:28.030
and you can actually
just play and play

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:32.360
whatever you need for
your science needs.

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:34.488
So this is the model
architecture they decided on.

00:26:34.488 --> 00:26:37.030
In the end, it's actually quite
similar to the exoplanet one.

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:41.320
It's a series of one-dimensional
convolutional layers

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:43.810
with MaxPooling, which were
then fed through some fully

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:47.320
connected layers at the end.

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:50.730
So this is a result from just a
random planet from their sample

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:51.900
only for five molecules.

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:53.400
So it's a bit hard
to see, but there

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:56.130
are some red stars
that are the true value

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.080
of that planetary atmosphere.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:00.000
And then the sort
of colorful blobs

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.985
are the predictions
from the model.

00:27:01.985 --> 00:27:03.360
And you're getting
a distribution

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:05.730
because this was actually
a Bayesian deep learning

00:27:05.730 --> 00:27:08.280
model that took advantage
of concrete dropout

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:09.980
to create these
posterior distributions.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:14.070
So you can see it does pretty
well for these molecules.

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:15.670
But it actually
predicts 12 molecules.

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:19.625
This is much more than other
planetary retrieval methods.

00:27:19.625 --> 00:27:21.000
And of course,
the big benefit is

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:23.790
that this is created in
seconds, whereas previously,

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:28.952
atmospheric retrieval requires
days to do the same thing.

00:27:28.952 --> 00:27:30.910
All right, so in the end,
the astrobiology team

00:27:30.910 --> 00:27:34.230
generated 3 million
rocky exoplanet spectra

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:38.580
and developed its first machine
learning atmospheric retrieval

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:41.910
model for rocky terrestrial
exoplanets, like the earth.

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:43.495
So this is pretty amazing.

00:27:43.495 --> 00:27:45.120
And it's all publicly
available, if you

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:47.810
wanted to play around with it.

00:27:47.810 --> 00:27:50.405
OK, so that's all
I had for you now.

00:27:50.405 --> 00:27:52.030
I've had a great time
sharing with you.

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:53.680
I hope you've had a great
time learning about some

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:56.222
of the cool science that can be
done through the Google Cloud

00:27:56.222 --> 00:27:57.250
Platform.

00:27:57.250 --> 00:27:58.750
And I just want to
finish by saying,

00:27:58.750 --> 00:28:00.292
this was an amazing
experience for me

00:28:00.292 --> 00:28:01.770
to go through this program.

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:03.760
Again, I had zero
machine learning

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:05.375
experience going into this.

00:28:05.375 --> 00:28:07.750
And the tools that are available
through the Google Cloud

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:11.380
Platform made it not just easy
to learn, but also enjoyable

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:12.220
and fun to learn.

00:28:12.220 --> 00:28:15.370
So I'm very excited to see
all the future science that

00:28:15.370 --> 00:28:18.790
can come out of collaborations
with the Google Cloud Platform.

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:19.720
Thank you very much.

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:21.220
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:25.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

